        {
          "_id": "qCVbO7rB516s2fZYKGSq",
          "name": "v1.0",
          "description": "Initial version",
          "parentVersionId": null,
          "todos": [],
          "errors": [
            {
              "title": "I couldnt add login through google",
              "id": 1770472592857,
              "description": "the error kept happening, so for now I am delaying this function, for use of common email and password thing. \nthe final instructions in chat was to install some Android studio SDK first, then to build and get SHA1 fingerprint.\n\nhe \"License Not Accepted\" error happens because Android Studio needs you to manually agree to the terms for the NDK (Native Development Kit).\n\nStep 1: Accept the Licenses\nRun this command in your terminal to accept all pending licenses. You will need to press y and Enter for each one:\n\npowershell\nC:\\Users\\nuraz\\Programs\\CodingSDKlibraryetcENV\\AndroidSDK\\cmdline-tools\\latest\\bin\\sdkmanager.bat --licenses\n(If that path is wrong, you can also do this in Android Studio: Settings -> Languages & Frameworks -> Android SDK -> SDK Tools -> Check \"NDK (Side by side)\" and \"Android SDK Command-line Tools\" -> Apply.)\n\nStep 2: The Fast Way (Skip Gradle)\nSince Gradle is trying to build the whole app just to show you a key, it's hitting those errors. You can bypass it entirely by using the keytool that comes with the Java you just installed.\n\nIMPORTANT: Restart your terminal window first (so it detects the new Java installation). Then run:\n\npowershell\nkeytool -list -v -keystore \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.android\\debug.keystore\" -alias androiddebugkey -storepass android -keypass android\nStep 3: If keytool is still \"Not Recognized\"\nIf it still says it can't find keytool, try running it using the full path to the Java you just installed:\n\npowershell\n& \"C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft\\jdk-17.0.14.7-hotspot\\bin\\keytool.exe\" -list -v -keystore \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.android\\debug.keystore\" -alias androiddebugkey -storepass android -keypass android\n(Note: Your version folder jdk-17... might be slightly different; you can check C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft to see the exact folder name.)\n\nOnce you get the SHA-1 from the output, copy it and add it to Firebase!",
              "createdAt": {
                "type": "firestore/timestamp/1.0",
                "seconds": 1770472592,
                "nanoseconds": 857000000
              },
              "resolved": false
            }
          ],
          "createdAt": "2026-02-01T13:04:22.816Z",
